It is said that in the future with one or more of the control, exclusivity and QIB provisions, Pacific National would be the sole provider of the relevant services in the Queensland market and the larger of two such providers in the interstate markets because:
Pacific National would have operational control of BMUT;
Aurizon would close QIB;
Aurizon has closed IIB; and
a party other than Pacific National would be unlikely to enter the relevant markets whilst Pacific National had operational control of BMUT, as Pacific National would have a strong incentive to operate BMUT in a way that favoured its own services and disadvantaged any other provider who may seek to use BMUT.

In my view, the ACCC has made out a prima facie case concerning:
the existence of the Principal Understanding, with the control provision and the exclusivity provision;
the purpose and likely anti-competitive effects arising therefrom;
the purpose and likely anti-competitive effect of provisions of the TSS;
the likely anti-competitive effect of the ART acquisition.

Fourth, Pacific National has recited a litany of criticisms as to the form of the injunction sought, even as proposed to be modified by the ACCC over the weekend. These include:
the difficulty if not impossibility of compliance;
the impact upon the ordinary conduct of its business;
the impact of the restraint on contracting;
the asymmetrical effect and burden that would be produced in the market place in that Pacific National would have a burden imposed upon it that Aurizon would not have in terms of normal competitive behaviour.
